# What reaches the Docker build context.
#
# The Dockerfile already COPYs only `src` and `config` into the runtime image,
# so this file is not what keeps the IMAGE small — it keeps the CONTEXT small,
# and that matters for two reasons:
#
# 1. **Secrets.** Everything here is uploaded to the Docker daemon before the
#    build starts. Without this file, a developer's `.env` — Kite API secret,
#    Anthropic key, Postgres password — is sent into the build context on every
#    build. It would not appear in the final image today, but it is one careless
#    `COPY . .` away from being baked into a layer, and layers are forever.
#
# 2. **Speed.** `.venv` alone is ~700 MB and `data/` grows without bound. Both
#    would be uploaded on every single build for no purpose.
#
# Deny-by-default is deliberate. Listing what to exclude means the next thing
# somebody adds is included by accident; listing what to include means the next
# thing is excluded by default and has to be added on purpose.

# --- exclude everything ---
*

# --- then re-admit only what the build genuinely needs ---
!src/
!config/
!pyproject.toml
!README.md

# --- and strip build noise back out of those ---
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/

# Never, under any circumstances:
#   .env, .venv, data/, tests/, .git — all already excluded by `*` above and
#   deliberately not re-admitted. The CI `image` job asserts their absence in
#   the built image rather than trusting this file.
